  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1116 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nStorms have ceased as of around 23z, likely due to increasing\nCINH from upper subsidence behind Friday's trough and surface air\nmass modification throughout the day. This will leave the region\ndry through Sunday morning. Patchy fog is still possible tonight\nwith high clouds keeping a higher coverage of fog at bay.\n\nSunday will see the start of this pesky heat wave once again\nregaining strength. NBM high temperatures have held steady with\npeak temperatures in the upper 90s areawide with some locations\npotentially reaching 100 degrees. Surface moisture is still\nabundant enough too to bring heat indices across portions of the\nregion along and west of the Mississippi River above 105 degrees.\nTherefore, we have issued a Heat Advisory from 11 AM through 7 PM\nSunday. For the vast majority of the area, PoPs are just about\nnon-existent, meaning that heat relief is not expected. However,\nsome short-term guidance hints that some outflow from potential\nstorms across Missouri could reach far northeastern Arkansas and\nthe Missouri Bootheel Sunday afternoon, but confidence is too\nlow. So, count on the heat sticking around through the day in\nthese areas.\n\nModels are in good agreement that the upper ridge responsible for\nthe increase in temperatures will persist through at least the\nmiddle of next week. The ridge will reach peak strength Tuesday\nwith forecast temperatures at or above 100 degrees for more than\nhalf the Mid-South both Tuesday and Wednesday. Again,\nprecipitation chances are just about nil through next week\nunderneath the ridge. So, heat relief is unlikely, but what is\nlikely is the need for additional heat headlines through at least\nWednesday.\n\nEnsembles have been hinting at a possible mechanism to remove the\nheat through the last several forecast cycles, primarily through\na deflection of the polar jet south with the high retrograding\nwest. However, this solution must have lost rapport within the\nensembles, because now they seem to prefer a cutoff low\ndeveloping over the Atlantic, originating from a shortwave\ncurrently over the Great Lakes. Members with this feature then\nbring it south and west along the Atlantic Coastline through\nmidweek. At the same time, a tropical wave also propagates\nthrough the Caribbean. The net effects of these features are a\ngrowing consensus of ensemble members depicting some form of\ntroughing over the Gulf or southeastern CONUS by late next week.\nAdditionally, from a climatological perspective, we are well\noverdue for some cooler temperatures, which should be on their\nway any week now. So, confidence, albeit slowly, is beginning to\nrise in a gradual weakening of the ridge late next week that\nshould hopefully yield lower temperatures. Lastly, although the\nmean background synoptics may see a weakening in the upper ridge,\nhigh forecast variability still exists in exact forcing\nmechanisms and atmospheric moisture. So, precipitation chances\nare uncertain for late next week and into next weekend for the\ntime being.",
